WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:14.780
Now I'd like to introduce today's feature
presenter, Professor Robert Sutton.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.451
Bob Sutton is the Professor
of Management Science and

00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:19.417
Engineering at Stanford University.

00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:22.804
He co-founded the Stanford
Technology Ventures Program and

00:00:22.804 --> 00:00:26.656
the Hasso Plattner Institute of
Designs also known as the d.school.

00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:29.831
He's a fellow at IDEO,
Senior Scientist at Gallup and

00:00:29.831 --> 00:00:31.911
an advisor to McKinsey &amp; Company.

00:00:31.911 --> 00:00:35.246
Bob studies organizational change,
leadership innovation, and

00:00:35.246 --> 00:00:36.364
workplace dynamics.

00:00:36.364 --> 00:00:41.699
He's published over 150 articles and
chapters and written seven books.

00:00:41.699 --> 00:00:43.695
Bob's latest book is The ****

00:00:43.695 --> 00:00:47.622
Survival Guide: How To Deal With
People Who Treat You Like Dirt.

00:00:47.622 --> 00:00:53.470
His website is bobsutton.net and
his Twitter handle is @work_matters.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.183
Now I'm going to turn it over to Bob.

00:00:57.183 --> 00:01:00.044
&gt;&gt; All right, thanks, Sherry.

00:01:00.044 --> 00:01:05.899
Great to be here, and I would like to
welcome everybody who's joining us.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.869
This is going to be kind of my first
major presentation of ideas in my book,

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:13.286
so I'm really excited about this.

00:01:13.286 --> 00:01:15.061
And so let's jump into it.

00:01:15.061 --> 00:01:20.271
And what I'm going to do is I'll talk for
25, 30 minutes or so.

00:01:20.271 --> 00:01:23.702
And then we'll open it up for
your questions and so

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:29.108
just keep those questions coming and
we'll answer as many as we possibly can.

00:01:29.108 --> 00:01:31.984
I love questions,
especially difficult ones.

00:01:31.984 --> 00:01:35.805
So ask me the hard ones, all right?

00:01:35.805 --> 00:01:40.345
So let me give you a little
context about both

00:01:40.345 --> 00:01:44.763
the story I'm going to be telling today,
and

00:01:44.763 --> 00:01:49.194
the book that I'm going to
be talking about.

00:01:49.194 --> 00:01:52.680
So about ten years ago,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.478
I accidentally got involved in
writing a book about workplace dirt.

00:01:57.478 --> 00:02:01.226
It's a book called the No **** Rule.

00:02:01.226 --> 00:02:03.539
And there are a couple of
strange things about that.

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:08.923
One, I never really considered
myself an expert on workplace jerks,

00:02:08.923 --> 00:02:11.215
but one thing led to another.

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:16.112
And things kind of reached the point where
I would go to professional events to talk

00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:19.290
about leadership, or
innovation, or something.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.029
And people for example,
McKinsey Consulting,

00:02:22.029 --> 00:02:24.644
they would introduce me
as this is Bob Sutton.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.478
He's done all this stuff, but really,
he'll always be the **** guy.

00:02:27.478 --> 00:02:31.049
So I got this kind of weird situation, and

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:35.783
I kind of resisted writing
kind of a sequel to the book.

00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:38.602
The reason that there was pressure for
a sequel,

00:02:38.602 --> 00:02:43.208
besides the fact that the first book sold
well, so they always wanted a sequel,

00:02:43.208 --> 00:02:47.130
is that the first book really
focused on organizational cultures,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.053
how to build an organizational culture
that was a relatively civilized workplace.

00:02:52.053 --> 00:02:56.522
But the response to it was just
about everybody I ever met

00:02:56.522 --> 00:03:01.088
at a cocktail party, my barber,
professional events,

00:03:01.088 --> 00:03:05.483
journalists, because I talk
to a lot of journalists.

00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:11.445
And I got 8,000 emails that all
essentially asked the same question,

00:03:11.445 --> 00:03:16.568
which was help, I'm dealing with
an **** or a bunch of them.

00:03:16.568 --> 00:03:17.612
What do I do?

00:03:17.612 --> 00:03:20.684
And the range of these
things was unbelievable.

00:03:20.684 --> 00:03:27.246
For example, a Costco worker
who had a boss who was a jerk.

00:03:27.246 --> 00:03:34.044
Another example of somebody who was
a jerk was a local CEO who described

00:03:34.044 --> 00:03:39.689
board-holes and
douche-boards who were driving him crazy.

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:45.575
I had a Baptist minister write me about
mean parishioners and what to do about it.

00:03:45.575 --> 00:03:48.002
And then a lot of questions about clients.

00:03:48.002 --> 00:03:50.170
And they kind of went over and over again.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:54.475
And so what I do while I worked on other
stuff such as leadership and scaling and

00:03:54.475 --> 00:03:56.275
innovation, stuff like that.

00:03:56.275 --> 00:04:00.325
I kind of keep a running folder of
all these different emails, and

00:04:00.325 --> 00:04:03.253
I said well,
maybe I'll get to them some day.

00:04:03.253 --> 00:04:07.834
And just to give you one more sort
of example, so here's a cartoon.

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:11.747
So one of the fun things we did for
this book is we had this cartoonist,

00:04:11.747 --> 00:04:16.562
David Willson, who's actually a political
cartoonist for a newspaper in Florida.

00:04:16.562 --> 00:04:22.962
And so he drew some cartoons based on
the new book, The **** Survival Guide.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:27.946
And I had this guy write me an email,
a really long email about how he

00:04:27.946 --> 00:04:33.199
spent seven years working for
what he called an $80 whole factoring

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:37.761
**** factory where there was
just meanness everywhere.

00:04:37.761 --> 00:04:39.148
There was a mean owner.

00:04:39.148 --> 00:04:43.084
He described, he turned mean and
he had trouble sleeping.

00:04:43.084 --> 00:04:44.846
He had physical health problems.

00:04:44.846 --> 00:04:49.763
He had trouble with his relationship
with the significant people in his life.

00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:54.353
And one of the favorite scenes, and
this is one of those weird things that

00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:57.297
jerky people do is
they're weird about food.

00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:02.438
So the picture there is what would happen
is that the owner would walk up to him and

00:05:02.438 --> 00:05:05.431
stick his hand into his
bags of potato chips and

00:05:05.431 --> 00:05:07.755
just eat them without permission.

00:05:07.755 --> 00:05:10.006
So these sort of questions and

00:05:10.006 --> 00:05:14.597
stories that I would hear were
kind of the focus of the book and

00:05:14.597 --> 00:05:19.550
what we're going to talk about in
the following 20 minutes or so,

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:25.790
are some of the lessons that I've learned
in this adventure over the last decade.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:30.630
To try to understand how to address really
this one question because this book just

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.166
addresses one question in a lot of ways.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.496
I'm dealing with a jerk, or a bunch
of them, especially in the workplace.

00:05:36.496 --> 00:05:38.707
What do I do about them?

00:05:38.707 --> 00:05:42.806
All right, so let me start out and

00:05:42.806 --> 00:05:48.225
talk a little bit in
addition to this big pile

00:05:48.225 --> 00:05:53.936
of e-mails that I was
getting over the years,

00:05:53.936 --> 00:05:59.795
the academic literature on all things ****

00:05:59.795 --> 00:06:04.500
absolutely exploded over the years.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.968
So and if you do a search of search
engines that are especially for

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:13.027
scholarly research,
things like abusive supervision,

00:06:13.027 --> 00:06:17.353
abusive customers, bullying,
workplace victimization.

00:06:17.353 --> 00:06:18.757
You can see the list there.

00:06:18.757 --> 00:06:22.039
It's just astounding how
much research there is,

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:25.028
that academics have
compiled over the years.

00:06:25.028 --> 00:06:28.849
And although we might want to
talk about this in the Q&amp;A,

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:33.974
there actually might be situations
where you individually might be able to

00:06:33.974 --> 00:06:38.957
get ahead at least for a little while
by treating other people like dirt.

00:06:38.957 --> 00:06:42.454
There's some controversy there about
what are the conditions under which,

00:06:42.454 --> 00:06:44.186
if you will, **** finish first.

00:06:44.186 --> 00:06:46.010
So there's not a lot.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:51.582
But the thing that comes through these
literally thousands of studies is that,

00:06:51.582 --> 00:06:54.587
if you are, if you will, in firing range.

00:06:54.587 --> 00:06:58.490
If you're around people who
treat others with disrespect,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.468
in a demeaning fashion,
who are rude, who do backstabbing,

00:07:02.468 --> 00:07:07.772
we can talk about some of the main, if you
will, kinds of dirty work that jerks do.

00:07:07.772 --> 00:07:10.695
It's all bad news,
thousands of studies show.

00:07:10.695 --> 00:07:15.425
So if you look on the left-hand side of
the slide, the evidence for well-being

00:07:15.425 --> 00:07:20.365
that if you are confronting, especially
over a long period of time, but even short

00:07:20.365 --> 00:07:25.307
little snippets, someone who leaves you
feeling bad about yourself, like dirt,

00:07:25.307 --> 00:07:30.059
anxiety, depression, poor relationships
with other people in your family.

00:07:30.059 --> 00:07:35.051
There's great longitudinal studies even
done in the UK that show that people

00:07:35.051 --> 00:07:39.678
who have nasty bosses have heart
problems and higher mortality rates.

00:07:39.678 --> 00:07:42.689
So that's the one side that for
you it's bad, and

00:07:42.689 --> 00:07:46.919
then in terms of stuff that will likely
be bad for your career and bad for

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:51.601
your employer, less productivity,
more errors, poor customer service.

00:07:51.601 --> 00:07:56.253
So what ends up happening is if your boss
and coworkers treat you like dirt You,

00:07:56.253 --> 00:08:01.379
in turn, it's say, well **** rolls
downhill, we'll treat clients bad as well.

00:08:01.379 --> 00:08:06.064
There's some great research in large
chains of fast food restaurants,

00:08:06.064 --> 00:08:10.902
that when the manager who runs
the restaurant treats the employees badly,

00:08:10.902 --> 00:08:15.007
they waste and steal more food,
and turnover, absenteeism.

00:08:15.007 --> 00:08:18.577
So regardless of maybe these conditions,

00:08:18.577 --> 00:08:24.337
which we could talk about when maybe
being a jerk helps you get ahead.

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:28.447
It's a pretty rare set of situations,
and probably overestimated.

00:08:28.447 --> 00:08:34.304
It's bad for your organization and bad for
your health to be around people like this.

00:08:34.304 --> 00:08:40.462
So that's a very clear message
from the academic research.

00:08:40.462 --> 00:08:41.710
One other thing, and

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:46.157
here's another one of these Dave Willson
cartoons that's also important.

00:08:46.157 --> 00:08:51.138
Another way in which it's bad for you is
that if you are around people who treat

00:08:51.138 --> 00:08:55.136
you with disrespect and jerks,
it's a contagious disease.

00:08:55.136 --> 00:08:59.596
It's a contagious disease in two ways
that come out of academic literature.

00:08:59.596 --> 00:09:04.275
One is, if you are around nasty people,
you are likely to catch it,

00:09:04.275 --> 00:09:06.010
just like a common cold.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.830
They can produce this in a laboratory.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:12.741
They bring in a subject, they treat him or
her like dirt, and they bring in

00:09:12.741 --> 00:09:18.144
another subject, and that person turns
into a dirt, turns into a nasty person.

00:09:18.144 --> 00:09:23.256
And there's also evidence the breed like
rabbits quotes, that there's this thing

00:09:23.256 --> 00:09:28.094
that a Harvard professor, a famous
Harvard professor named Rosabeth Kanter.

00:09:28.094 --> 00:09:31.108
She described it as
homosocial reproduction,

00:09:31.108 --> 00:09:36.078
that organizations tend to pick people
who are just like them, so literally,

00:09:36.078 --> 00:09:38.435
jerks are more likely to hire jerks.

00:09:38.435 --> 00:09:42.592
So if you hire one or two,
be careful they will breed like rabbits.

00:09:42.592 --> 00:09:47.173
All right, so what we're going to do
is we're going to start moving to

00:09:47.173 --> 00:09:50.337
the survival part of the show if you will,
but

00:09:50.337 --> 00:09:54.526
before I do, let me give you
a little mental provisioning.

00:09:54.526 --> 00:09:59.445
And something that really I've learned,
especially over the last decade,

00:09:59.445 --> 00:10:04.289
both from the academic research,
from talking to people who are jerks, and

00:10:04.289 --> 00:10:08.828
honestly, when I've been a jerk myself,
too, we human beings have so

00:10:08.828 --> 00:10:10.740
many self-serving biases.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:15.042
We're so bad at seeing our weaknesses,
that including for

00:10:15.042 --> 00:10:19.774
things like disrespect or ****,
that we're not very good at

00:10:19.774 --> 00:10:24.249
recognizing when we've treated
others with disrespect, or

00:10:24.249 --> 00:10:28.981
are consistently disrespectful,
or nasty, but on top of that,

00:10:28.981 --> 00:10:33.742
we're pretty quick to blame other
forces outside of ourselves.

00:10:33.742 --> 00:10:39.154
So if you want to kind of, if you will,
mental provisioning a mantra as you're

00:10:39.154 --> 00:10:44.648
dealing with managing situations where
you feel as if you're being demeaned,

00:10:44.648 --> 00:10:47.613
de-energized, oppressed, whatever.

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:51.407
I would sort of suggest be slow
to label others as ****, but

00:10:51.407 --> 00:10:55.417
be quick to label yourself as one,
because you're, by doing so,

00:10:55.417 --> 00:10:59.716
you're counter veiling a whole bunch
of other psychological forces,

00:10:59.716 --> 00:11:04.401
well documented psychological forces,
that push you the other direction.

00:11:04.401 --> 00:11:10.568
Okay, so here's my four main
steps of survival methods.

00:11:10.568 --> 00:11:12.857
These are chapters in
the **** survival guide.

00:11:12.857 --> 00:11:16.815
So let's sort of jump in and
start talking about them.

00:11:16.815 --> 00:11:20.428
The first one, the best thing you
can do is make a clean getaway.

00:11:20.428 --> 00:11:24.128
If you are in a situation,
it could be a customer,

00:11:24.128 --> 00:11:27.487
we'll talk about customers
in a little bit.

00:11:27.487 --> 00:11:29.908
It could be a co-worker.

00:11:29.908 --> 00:11:31.302
It could be a boss.

00:11:31.302 --> 00:11:36.301
It could be a Lord of the Flies situation
where everybody treats everybody else

00:11:36.301 --> 00:11:37.067
like dirt.

00:11:37.067 --> 00:11:41.868
Doing the best you can to get out as
quickly as you can is really smart, and

00:11:41.868 --> 00:11:43.781
I've got Johnny Paycheck.

00:11:43.781 --> 00:11:47.059
You may have heard the famous
Johnny Paycheck song,

00:11:47.059 --> 00:11:51.081
Take This Job and Shove It, but
my reaction is, yes do this, but

00:11:51.081 --> 00:11:56.020
don't be stupid about it in such a way
that it particularly ruins your career.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.486
And your reputation, and
just one little example,

00:11:59.486 --> 00:12:04.445
I suspect many of you have heard of,
is back in 2010 there was a guy named

00:12:04.445 --> 00:12:08.018
Steven Slater who was briefly
an American folk hero.

00:12:08.018 --> 00:12:12.678
So what happened was, he's a flight
attendant on JetBlue Airlines, and

00:12:12.678 --> 00:12:15.157
he has two legs with a couple of really,

00:12:15.157 --> 00:12:19.235
really obnoxious female passengers
who are just berating him.

00:12:19.235 --> 00:12:22.533
So he lands the second time,
I believe it was in Pittsburg, and

00:12:22.533 --> 00:12:27.078
one of the folks who'd been berating him,
one of the customers, she stands up early,

00:12:27.078 --> 00:12:30.690
while the plane's still taxiing,
opens the overhead luggage, and

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.956
he runs up to stop her, and
he gets hit on the head really hard.

00:12:33.956 --> 00:12:39.010
So he's pissed, and he's been abused
by these people for a few hours.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:42.148
So what he does is, famously,
he gets on the microphone,

00:12:42.148 --> 00:12:45.030
cusses them all out,
tells them they're all jerks,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:48.828
says he quits, and that's where you
see the slide, grabs two beers.

00:12:48.828 --> 00:12:53.259
And activates the slide, and
runs away from the airplane, and so,

00:12:53.259 --> 00:12:57.299
if you want a made for media,
take this job and shove it story,

00:12:57.299 --> 00:13:01.843
this is perfect, but let's go back
to the don't be stupid about it.

00:13:01.843 --> 00:13:03.436
He expressed regret later.

00:13:03.436 --> 00:13:04.640
He got fired.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:05.730
He got fined.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:07.284
He was on probation for a year.

00:13:07.284 --> 00:13:11.836
So it's one of those things that
might feel good in the moment, and

00:13:11.836 --> 00:13:16.470
as somebody who has sometimes has
limited emotional self control,

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.314
I know it's often good to have a plan, and

00:13:19.314 --> 00:13:23.405
that's one of my general models and
mantras in the book to.

00:13:23.405 --> 00:13:27.676
Back to, it's good to have a plan,
is that, I wish I could just give you

00:13:27.676 --> 00:13:32.519
a formula or a decision tree that if you
have a jerk, if you do these three things,

00:13:32.519 --> 00:13:37.222
everything's going to be great, but
unfortunately we all are in such different

00:13:37.222 --> 00:13:41.178
situations, that I can't give you
a one size fits all solution.

00:13:41.178 --> 00:13:46.159
So each one of us, if you will,
has to craft our own **** survival plan

00:13:46.159 --> 00:13:51.484
based on what we know, and in consultation
with people who we know and trust.

00:13:51.484 --> 00:13:58.186
All right, so one other thing, so
I'm talking about making a clean getaway.

00:13:58.186 --> 00:14:02.903
Even better than that is, when you're
going through the interview process,

00:14:02.903 --> 00:14:07.617
the job search process, any sorts of
information you can get that you are about

00:14:07.617 --> 00:14:11.475
to get involved with somebody who
is going to treat you like dirt and

00:14:11.475 --> 00:14:14.931
be disrespectful,
it's really important to look for.

00:14:14.931 --> 00:14:18.051
Of course, you can do things
like read Glass Door online,

00:14:18.051 --> 00:14:19.644
which isn't that reliable,

00:14:19.644 --> 00:14:23.609
because you don't know what part of the
organization you're going to end up in.

00:14:23.609 --> 00:14:27.858
Socially positive gossip's
really important.

00:14:27.858 --> 00:14:32.363
If you have friends, or people you can get
to, friends of friends of friends, who

00:14:32.363 --> 00:14:37.001
work in the work group you're going to go
to, that's probably the best information,

00:14:37.001 --> 00:14:40.884
and if you can have some early meetings,
you might look for red flags.

00:14:40.884 --> 00:14:45.269
And just the example I'm going to talk
about, so the reason I've got a hippo

00:14:45.269 --> 00:14:49.938
there is if you think about hippos, hippos
have a big mouth and they have little

00:14:49.938 --> 00:14:54.059
ears, so that's one way in which
hippos are difficult to work with.

00:14:54.059 --> 00:14:58.155
Also, there's another meaning of hippos,
some of you probably heard of, which is

00:14:58.155 --> 00:15:02.038
the highest paid person in the room, and
those two things usually go together.

00:15:02.038 --> 00:15:05.789
So anyhow, this it eight or
ten years ago, with one of my co-authors,

00:15:05.789 --> 00:15:09.978
who've I've actually done a lot with
at Stanford, at the Stanford Center for

00:15:09.978 --> 00:15:13.544
Professional Development, and
we've written books together and

00:15:13.544 --> 00:15:14.630
stuff, Huggy Rao.

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:18.186
So, Huggy and I had this
potential consulting client, and

00:15:18.186 --> 00:15:20.958
we flew to Boston for
an all-day meeting, and

00:15:20.958 --> 00:15:25.173
we're trying to figure out how much
to work with this guy and his team.

00:15:25.173 --> 00:15:28.892
So we're in this conference room for
ten hours, nine hours,

00:15:28.892 --> 00:15:33.467
one of those horrible all day meetings,
and the guy who is head of the group,

00:15:33.467 --> 00:15:38.112
he talked, and he talked, and he talked,
and he talked, and he talked, and

00:15:38.112 --> 00:15:41.720
he only made statements,
he never asked us any questions.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:46.023
He talked, and he talked, and it's about 2
o'clock, and so finally he sits down next

00:15:46.023 --> 00:15:50.345
to me, and he was actually silent, and
somebody else talked And he leans over and

00:15:50.345 --> 00:15:54.490
he looks at me, and he says, and this is
the quote, it looks like I'm listening,

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:58.481
but I'm really just reloading, trying to
figure out what I'm going to do next.

00:15:58.481 --> 00:16:00.453
So after that meeting, Heidi and

00:16:00.453 --> 00:16:04.967
I very quickly announced to our client
that we were too busy to work with him.

00:16:04.967 --> 00:16:10.013
And I think that saved us a lot
of aggravation, all right.

00:16:10.013 --> 00:16:15.044
The next set of approaches, so
if you're in a situation where

00:16:15.044 --> 00:16:19.785
you actually can't avoid,
if you will, working with for

00:16:19.785 --> 00:16:24.427
a short or a long period of
time with people who are jerks,

00:16:24.427 --> 00:16:30.653
the way that I think about it is that
assholeness is sort of like kryptonite.

00:16:30.653 --> 00:16:35.519
All sorts of evidence that the closer you
are to this nasty stuff, the more frequent

00:16:35.519 --> 00:16:39.921
your exposure, the more intense exposure,
the worse your physical and

00:16:39.921 --> 00:16:44.159
mental health will be, and also the more
likely you are turn into a jerk.

00:16:44.159 --> 00:16:49.440
So this is my colleague Katy DeCelles,
that's actually her picture on CNN.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:55.098
And she visited Stanford a couple years
ago, and she studies all things ****.

00:16:55.098 --> 00:16:56.374
She studies air rage.

00:16:56.374 --> 00:16:59.315
She studies the relationship between
prison guards and prisoners.

00:16:59.315 --> 00:17:05.247
She studies temper tantrums by basketball
coaches to see the effects of it.

00:17:05.247 --> 00:17:08.024
So it was really great
having her next door to me.

00:17:08.024 --> 00:17:11.232
And I said to Katy at some point
working on the book, so Katie,

00:17:11.232 --> 00:17:14.276
what's the most important
single bit of advice you have?

00:17:14.276 --> 00:17:16.704
And I kind of like her,
don't engage with crazy.

00:17:16.704 --> 00:17:20.844
And she means, create as much distance
from crazy as you possibly can,

00:17:20.844 --> 00:17:22.709
given the reality of your life.

00:17:22.709 --> 00:17:27.584
Okay, so let me give you an example
of something that I learned from

00:17:27.584 --> 00:17:32.388
a former doctoral student I know
about how to limit the exposure.

00:17:32.388 --> 00:17:37.258
And to me this is a really important
powerful method when you can't make

00:17:37.258 --> 00:17:38.325
a full escape.

00:17:38.325 --> 00:17:43.833
This idea of sort of slowing the rhythm to
reduce the contact, and in this instant

00:17:43.833 --> 00:17:48.858
world we're in, we get emails and
texts and stuff all hours of the night.

00:17:48.858 --> 00:17:50.708
People can send them repeatedly.

00:17:50.708 --> 00:17:53.168
Phone calls, meetings and so on.

00:17:53.168 --> 00:17:57.116
And what this doctoral student
did was she figured out,

00:17:57.116 --> 00:18:01.066
as said,
that she had a batshit crazy advisor.

00:18:01.066 --> 00:18:04.397
He would do things like call her at
3 in the morning and yell at her and

00:18:04.397 --> 00:18:05.044
rant at her.

00:18:05.044 --> 00:18:08.632
Write her repeated one email
after another, after another,

00:18:08.632 --> 00:18:11.258
often very critical and
often very hostile.

00:18:11.258 --> 00:18:14.531
And she's kind of stuck with
this dissertation advisor and

00:18:14.531 --> 00:18:16.435
is trying to figure out what to do.

00:18:16.435 --> 00:18:22.063
And she starts realizing that for him,
and there are certain sorts of ****,

00:18:22.063 --> 00:18:27.793
especially Machiavellian ****,
if you will, your pain is their pleasure.

00:18:27.793 --> 00:18:30.888
When they see you,
that really long explanatory email,

00:18:30.888 --> 00:18:33.402
they see you suffering,
they see you crying,

00:18:33.402 --> 00:18:37.479
and they see you squirming, the pleasure
centers in their brain light up.

00:18:37.479 --> 00:18:39.902
And this is the kind of
**** she was dealing with.

00:18:39.902 --> 00:18:45.154
So what she learned to do
was to slow the rhythm.

00:18:45.154 --> 00:18:50.090
So if he called, she wouldn't answer it,
or she would answer every fifth or

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:50.948
sixth call.

00:18:50.948 --> 00:18:55.358
If he sent a nasty email,
she'd wait for the next nasty email,

00:18:55.358 --> 00:18:56.950
the next nasty email.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.734
And the first year or so
she'd wait a couple days.

00:18:59.734 --> 00:19:04.195
And by the time she finished her PhD,
she'd wait two or three weeks and

00:19:04.195 --> 00:19:08.822
just send one sort of neutral email
response and wait another few weeks.

00:19:08.822 --> 00:19:10.131
Same thing with meetings.

00:19:10.131 --> 00:19:14.633
She had the meeting stretched first
weekly, bi-weekly, then monthly.

00:19:14.633 --> 00:19:19.285
And this idea of slowing the rhythm
is one of the things that you

00:19:19.285 --> 00:19:23.509
might be able to do within
the constraints of a real job.

00:19:23.509 --> 00:19:26.971
Another thing, and I didn't put in
this step, but it's really important.

00:19:26.971 --> 00:19:29.674
There's really great research,
especially for

00:19:29.674 --> 00:19:32.517
those of you who work in
an open office environment.

00:19:32.517 --> 00:19:34.802
If you can get a little bit away,

00:19:34.802 --> 00:19:40.657
physically far away from the jerks in your
office, it has a huge positive effect.

00:19:40.657 --> 00:19:44.860
In fact, there's a great long-term
study that was just published,

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:48.495
it was described in
the Harvard Business Review and places,

00:19:48.495 --> 00:19:52.627
that shows that when people sit
within 25 feet of a toxic coworker,

00:19:52.627 --> 00:19:55.931
the odds that they're going to
turn into a jerk go way up.

00:19:55.931 --> 00:19:57.873
And so
are the odds they're going to be fired.

00:19:57.873 --> 00:20:01.947
So trying to get that extra 10 or
15 feet will have a big effect.

00:20:01.947 --> 00:20:06.666
Another thing that you can do to reduce
the exposure is to find a safety zone,

00:20:06.666 --> 00:20:11.412
a place you can hide where the ****
can't get you, at least for a while.

00:20:11.412 --> 00:20:17.430
And the reason I have this crazy Old David
Willson cartoon is this goes back to,

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:20.685
it was so
long ago I had a full head of hair.

00:20:20.685 --> 00:20:22.170
This was like in the 1980s.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:23.821
This was a really long time ago.

00:20:23.821 --> 00:20:26.411
And my colleague,
Dan Denison and I in Michigan,

00:20:26.411 --> 00:20:28.939
we were studying a group
of surgical nurses, and

00:20:28.939 --> 00:20:33.090
we're standing there in the operating
room, operating theater, whatever.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.279
And we're talking to the head nurse.

00:20:34.279 --> 00:20:37.668
And she describes this doctor who
had a history of sexual harassment.

00:20:37.668 --> 00:20:38.917
It was a serious problem.

00:20:38.917 --> 00:20:43.220
As she describes it,
this female nurse runs down the hall with

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:46.793
this aforementioned doctor
grabbing her behind.

00:20:46.793 --> 00:20:49.193
So she looks it up and goes, see?

00:20:49.193 --> 00:20:54.081
Well, a few minutes later, her and
a number of the other female nurses went

00:20:54.081 --> 00:20:57.895
into the nurses' lounge, and
we tried to follow them in.

00:20:57.895 --> 00:20:59.169
She put up her hand.

00:20:59.169 --> 00:21:02.777
You can't go near us in a nursing
room in the nurses' lounge.

00:21:02.777 --> 00:21:04.402
This is our territory for safety.

00:21:04.402 --> 00:21:09.159
So finding a place to hide can be useful.

00:21:09.159 --> 00:21:10.780
Use a human shield.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:13.004
This is what a boss is for.

00:21:13.004 --> 00:21:17.826
If you get a great boss, what a great
boss does, and there's research on one of

00:21:17.826 --> 00:21:23.109
the functions of management is to buffer
employees from disturbance and annoyance.

00:21:23.109 --> 00:21:27.362
If you can find a boss who serves as
a human shield, that's another way to deal

00:21:27.362 --> 00:21:31.303
with the ****, so that's picking
your boss is really important.

00:21:31.303 --> 00:21:36.124
So this, I love this quote from a sports
director who wrote me some years ago,

00:21:36.124 --> 00:21:38.065
university sports director.

00:21:38.065 --> 00:21:40.626
I always tell the people who work for
me the same thing.

00:21:40.626 --> 00:21:44.394
My job is to hold the umbrella, so
the **** from above doesn't hit you.

00:21:44.394 --> 00:21:47.209
Your job is to keep me
from having to use it.

00:21:47.209 --> 00:21:51.658
So that's such a symbiotic relationship.

00:21:51.658 --> 00:21:56.351
All right, one other thing that I kind of
got obsessed with is this notion that when

00:21:56.351 --> 00:22:00.494
there's work groups, especially
when there's sort of a jerky boss,

00:22:00.494 --> 00:22:05.189
that sometimes there's sort of early
warning systems where people formally and

00:22:05.189 --> 00:22:09.701
informally create social connections
to warn that the nasty boss is coming.

00:22:09.701 --> 00:22:13.572
Or that the boss is in
a particularly bad mood.

00:22:13.572 --> 00:22:17.196
I've actually got a lot of emails about
this, and there's stories in the press.

00:22:17.196 --> 00:22:22.854
But a great example of this is an old, not
that old, Sandra Bullock movie where she

00:22:22.854 --> 00:22:28.353
plays a really nasty **** publisher who
is despised by all of her coworkers.

00:22:28.353 --> 00:22:30.862
She runs around and yells at people,
and belittles people.

00:22:30.862 --> 00:22:37.058
And so they had an early warning system,
so the movie is called The Proposal.

00:22:37.058 --> 00:22:41.791
So what happened was that her
assistant figured out that she was

00:22:41.791 --> 00:22:46.624
going to be coming and
visiting another building, another room.

00:22:46.624 --> 00:22:49.929
So that's what the email that was sent,
the witch is on her broom.

00:22:49.929 --> 00:22:54.766
This sort of warning that,
if you will, there's an incoming ****.

00:22:54.766 --> 00:22:57.300
So sometimes those things can help.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:03.802
All right, I think that's
enough avoidance techniques.

00:23:03.802 --> 00:23:07.526
Now let me talk about ones
that are really powerful for

00:23:07.526 --> 00:23:12.908
reducing the negative effect of somebody
who treats you like dirt on yourself

00:23:12.908 --> 00:23:18.236
without really changing the asshole's
behavior or your situation at all.

00:23:18.236 --> 00:23:20.326
So this is Dr. Aaron Beck.

00:23:20.326 --> 00:23:25.103
He's the modern founder of something
called cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:23:25.103 --> 00:23:29.744
which is the most widely used evidence
based therapy actually in the world.

00:23:29.744 --> 00:23:33.945
And a lot of what happens in cognitive
behavioral therapy is, rather than

00:23:33.945 --> 00:23:38.910
just changing your situation, you reframe
and essentially change your perception.

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:42.522
So that things that are driving
you crazy and making you sick and

00:23:42.522 --> 00:23:47.380
making you anxious, you reframe them so
they're less upsetting, even good things.

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:50.824
So, let me give you a few
examples that are kind of

00:23:50.824 --> 00:23:54.522
loosely drawn from cognitive
behavioral therapy.

00:23:54.522 --> 00:23:59.556
So one thing that happens in cognitive
behavioral therapy, you'll recode

00:23:59.556 --> 00:24:04.447
the threat as less nasty, threatening,
upsetting than you did before.

00:24:04.447 --> 00:24:09.016
So let me give you a couple of examples
of where I've seen this is my work and

00:24:09.016 --> 00:24:09.919
my research.

00:24:09.919 --> 00:24:13.491
So, one of the heroes of my book,
her name is Becky Margiotta.

00:24:13.491 --> 00:24:16.111
Becky's just a magnificent human being,

00:24:16.111 --> 00:24:20.560
one of the most accomplished social
activists I've ever met in my life.

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:25.418
Becky actually led a campaign called The
100,000 Homes Campaign that found homes

00:24:25.418 --> 00:24:28.281
for more than 100,000 homeless Americans.

00:24:28.281 --> 00:24:31.900
She's a woman who can get stuff done.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:35.987
So Becky in the 1980s went to West Point,
the US Military Academy.

00:24:35.987 --> 00:24:39.640
She was one of the get women in
the early days at West Point.

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.857
And if you know anything about West Point
or in fact any military academy,

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:46.171
there's a lot of hazing the first year.

00:24:46.171 --> 00:24:51.700
So Becky, she's a first year plebe, and
the typical thing that would happen,

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:57.060
I love the way she describes it, is that
so it's 9 o'clock in the morning and

00:24:57.060 --> 00:25:01.598
an upper class cadet walks up to her and
gets in front of her nose and

00:25:01.598 --> 00:25:05.999
says, repeat the major headlines
of the New York Times today.

00:25:05.999 --> 00:25:06.685
This morning.

00:25:06.685 --> 00:25:08.149
This is a standard hazing.

00:25:08.149 --> 00:25:11.101
So she said you go and you try and repeat
it and of course you got it wrong and

00:25:11.101 --> 00:25:14.360
they'd scream at you and tell you what
an idiot you were one inch from your nose.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:17.540
So she said the first couple
of weeks she'd get all upset.

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:21.975
But then she came out with the strategy of
just seeing them as this sort of brilliant

00:25:21.975 --> 00:25:24.050
people who were really really funny.

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:26.054
Who really weren't hurting her,

00:25:26.054 --> 00:25:29.794
they were sort of a performance
she was watching in the theater.

00:25:29.794 --> 00:25:33.739
And you can see, this idea sort
of not having it be that bad.

00:25:33.739 --> 00:25:36.426
And that's one of the things
that helped her get through and

00:25:36.426 --> 00:25:37.784
she became very successful.

00:25:37.784 --> 00:25:39.583
She was involved in special operations.

00:25:39.583 --> 00:25:42.491
Had a very successful military career.

00:25:42.491 --> 00:25:45.138
Bill collectors, years ago in the 90s,

00:25:45.138 --> 00:25:49.413
I did some anthropological research
on telephone bill collectors.

00:25:49.413 --> 00:25:53.252
The people who call us when you're
late on your Visa and MasterCard.

00:25:53.252 --> 00:25:57.817
And one thing that we were taught, when we
get a screamer, somebody would threaten

00:25:57.817 --> 00:26:01.795
us, they would yell at us, a lot of
the whole culture was, afterwards,

00:26:01.795 --> 00:26:04.168
you debrief with your fellow collectors.

00:26:04.168 --> 00:26:07.210
And they would all say the same thing,
that's nothing,

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.271
you should have seen what happened to me.

00:26:09.271 --> 00:26:11.370
Standard sort of solution.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:12.400
Okay, that's one.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:17.456
Another one, one I kind of like,
is when somebody is nasty and

00:26:17.456 --> 00:26:20.431
abusive and all this sort of stuff,

00:26:20.431 --> 00:26:25.586
rather than to sort of lessen
the degree to which it hurts you,

00:26:25.586 --> 00:26:32.550
is rather than getting angry with them,
then feel sorry for them and forgive them.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.980
So, some of the expressions here as
you say to yourself if you've got

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:36.700
a jerky boss.

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:38.875
She's like a porcupine
with a heart of gold.

00:26:38.875 --> 00:26:41.312
Maybe there's some good in there.

00:26:41.312 --> 00:26:45.951
I heard this on Google years ago, he's
like the guy with a bad user interface but

00:26:45.951 --> 00:26:47.485
a good operating system.

00:26:47.485 --> 00:26:51.833
And the key thing here about having
sympathy, having forgiveness for

00:26:51.833 --> 00:26:56.403
somebody who treats you badly is even
if you don't change your behavior,

00:26:56.403 --> 00:27:00.530
there's all sorts of evidence that
when you can forgive people and

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:05.345
have some distance, it does less harm
to you because you don't ruminate it.

00:27:05.345 --> 00:27:07.213
You're not obsessed with it.

00:27:07.213 --> 00:27:11.802
So in some ways, one way to blunt
the effect of an **** who even

00:27:11.802 --> 00:27:15.458
wants to hurt you is to
forgive that person anyway.

00:27:18.386 --> 00:27:23.452
Another one which is kind of related,
this is the Michelle Obama,

00:27:23.452 --> 00:27:26.623
when they go low we go high, philosophy.

00:27:26.623 --> 00:27:29.360
Is this notion that,
when people treat you like dirt.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:32.953
That you rise above it and say you're
not going to stoop to that level.

00:27:32.953 --> 00:27:36.305
And when we're in the course of
doing the research for the book,

00:27:36.305 --> 00:27:38.178
I ran into a guy named Jacob Jabber.

00:27:38.178 --> 00:27:42.313
Jacob Jabber is a CEO of a coffee chain
that's mostly out here on the West Coast

00:27:42.313 --> 00:27:43.663
called Phil's Coffee.

00:27:43.663 --> 00:27:47.587
They're infiltrating other places
like Washington, DC and the like.

00:27:47.587 --> 00:27:51.755
And they're really into this notion that
they make you this custom cup of coffee.

00:27:51.755 --> 00:27:54.903
And you have this relationship
with your barista.

00:27:54.903 --> 00:27:56.814
Jacob calls it cups of love.

00:27:56.814 --> 00:28:02.771
And he said his perspective was, our view
is that if a customer is nasty to you,

00:28:02.771 --> 00:28:05.583
you throw the kindness back to them.

00:28:05.583 --> 00:28:07.162
You kill them with kindness.

00:28:07.162 --> 00:28:10.154
So one of my students,
I hired her and she went out and

00:28:10.154 --> 00:28:14.237
did a bunch of interviews with Phil's
coffee folks, and sure enough,

00:28:14.237 --> 00:28:18.251
you can see the quotes here,
their whole culture was, essentially,

00:28:18.251 --> 00:28:22.360
to not to stoop to the level of customers
and to kill them with kindness.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:25.074
Because acting like that is beneath them.

00:28:25.074 --> 00:28:28.544
So that's another kind
of interesting strategy.

00:28:28.544 --> 00:28:32.039
Then two more, two of my favorite ones.

00:28:32.039 --> 00:28:37.032
This one probably is one of the strongest
evidenced based approaches if you're

00:28:37.032 --> 00:28:41.876
dealing with somebody who's a jerk,
to create some distance between you and

00:28:41.876 --> 00:28:46.820
them to a little imaginary time travel,
they call this temporal distancing.

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:51.495
There's three laboratory based studies
that show whether it's something like

00:28:51.495 --> 00:28:55.895
breaking up from a long term relationship,
having trouble with the tests,

00:28:55.895 --> 00:29:00.567
that people who think about it in terms of
how they're going to feel about it a week

00:29:00.567 --> 00:29:03.344
from now, a month from now,
or a year from now.

00:29:03.344 --> 00:29:05.996
Rather than focusing
on how upset they are,

00:29:05.996 --> 00:29:10.090
they tend to suffer much less upset
anxiety, depression and so on.

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:14.512
And when it comes to **** this is one
of the things I would see over and

00:29:14.512 --> 00:29:18.200
over again as a coping strategy and
one of my favorite ones,

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:21.334
I guess I'm doing a lot
of military academy ones.

00:29:21.334 --> 00:29:26.205
Is the guy wrote me when he attended the
air force academy and he was being hazed,

00:29:26.205 --> 00:29:29.854
because we know this is part of the thing,
you're being hazed.

00:29:29.854 --> 00:29:34.216
He said he would look past them, and he's
imagined it was three years from then and

00:29:34.216 --> 00:29:35.685
he was in his plane flying.

00:29:35.685 --> 00:29:39.697
And this was nothing to get through to
be able to have the privilege of flying

00:29:39.697 --> 00:29:41.558
the plane, and it worked for him.

00:29:41.558 --> 00:29:45.041
So a little imaginary time
travel can be very powerful.

00:29:45.041 --> 00:29:49.682
Finally, in a lot of what I'm talking
about, there's ways to become more

00:29:49.682 --> 00:29:54.770
emotionally detached and distant from
the person who's treating you like dirt,

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:59.733
and they call these reframing sometimes,
emotional detachment strategies.

00:29:59.733 --> 00:30:04.041
They're called all sorts of ways and you
can sort of see it in that air force plea

00:30:04.041 --> 00:30:06.217
trainee that I was just talking about.

00:30:06.217 --> 00:30:08.909
He would find a way to do
emotional distance, but

00:30:08.909 --> 00:30:12.787
one of my favorite ones comes actually
from a Stanford administrator I

00:30:12.787 --> 00:30:17.255
know who is one of the most serene people
at dealing with well, I hate to admit it,

00:30:17.255 --> 00:30:21.462
we're sitting here at Stanford around
the table with multiple people, but

00:30:21.462 --> 00:30:22.942
we do have a few ****.

00:30:22.942 --> 00:30:26.184
Maybe fewer than other organizations but
we got them.

00:30:26.184 --> 00:30:30.761
So anyhow, so what this guy does, I almost
used his name, that would be terrible.

