But let me just tell you about
the evidence about the best way

00:44:20.829 --> 00:44:25.345
to fight an **** boss,
documentation and form a posse.

00:44:25.345 --> 00:44:29.351
All sorts of evidence, good anti-bullying
studies is the more people on

00:44:29.351 --> 00:44:33.503
your side and the more documentation
you have, that we get social support.

00:44:33.503 --> 00:44:37.711
And if you just go and complain
about your boss, well, I don't know.

00:44:37.711 --> 00:44:39.886
If you're Gretchen Carlson,
you got the recording,

00:44:39.886 --> 00:44:41.740
that might be enough in
the big-time lawyer.

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:43.673
But for most of us,
that's not going to work.

00:44:43.673 --> 00:44:50.003
And one of my favorite examples of this
was an email somebody wrote me years ago.

00:44:50.003 --> 00:44:53.848
It was an animal control officer and
they had a member, a fellow animal control

00:44:53.848 --> 00:44:57.823
officer constantly abusing them,
swearing at them, making racist comments.

00:44:57.823 --> 00:45:00.210
They go to the boss, they complain,
nothing would happen.

00:45:00.210 --> 00:45:02.031
So what they did was they formed,

00:45:02.031 --> 00:45:05.163
they put together what they
called the **** diaries.

00:45:05.163 --> 00:45:08.600
Each one of them for a two-week
period just recorded, with dates,

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:12.362
all the different incidents and
all the horrible things this person did.

00:45:12.362 --> 00:45:15.917
As a group, the five or
six of them went to the boss.

00:45:15.917 --> 00:45:18.615
And then that person
was gone within a week.

00:45:18.615 --> 00:45:23.424
And that doesn't usually happen, but there
you've got this idea of documentation and

00:45:23.424 --> 00:45:23.946
a posse.

00:45:23.946 --> 00:45:26.140
And a lot of it does depend
on the organization.

00:45:26.140 --> 00:45:29.247
There are some organization where
you can go to your boss's boss, but

00:45:29.247 --> 00:45:31.682
you damn well better know
the political environment.

00:45:31.682 --> 00:45:34.687
That's a dangerous thing to
do in some organizations.

00:45:34.687 --> 00:45:38.114
&gt;&gt; Advice, Bob, Here's a good question.

00:45:38.114 --> 00:45:41.033
Many of our participants work
in distributed global teams.

00:45:41.033 --> 00:45:44.611
Can you give suggestions for managing
jerks when you're not face to face?

00:45:44.611 --> 00:45:48.956
&gt;&gt; Yes, and in fact Pam Hinds,
who is one of our fellow faculty members.

00:45:48.956 --> 00:45:51.610
She's got an I&amp;E, one or
our new executive programs,

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:53.655
she's one of the world's expert on this.

00:45:53.655 --> 00:45:56.626
So everything I know about this
I've learned from Pam Hinds, so

00:45:56.626 --> 00:45:57.960
look at Pam Hinds's stuff.

00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:01.885
So here is what the research
on distributed team.

00:46:01.885 --> 00:46:04.088
And in fact,
even if you're in the same big room and

00:46:04.088 --> 00:46:07.016
you're working on slack all day and
you're not talking to people,

00:46:07.016 --> 00:46:09.861
it's just as bad as if people
are in other countries, by the way.

00:46:09.861 --> 00:46:14.178
So to the extent that you don't
have eye contact with people and

00:46:14.178 --> 00:46:19.897
you don't understand the context therein,
you are more likely to have problems.

00:46:19.897 --> 00:46:23.539
So the best thing you can do, and
Pam Hinds has all sorts of evidence.

00:46:23.539 --> 00:46:25.444
If you're in a distributed team,

00:46:25.444 --> 00:46:29.649
especially at the beginning of the
project, if you can meet them even once,

00:46:29.649 --> 00:46:33.152
face to face, before the project begins,
that really helps.

00:46:33.152 --> 00:46:37.453
And the other kind of stuff that Pam talks
about when you do stuff that's distributed

00:46:37.453 --> 00:46:40.129
is it seems like it's wasting time,
but it's not.

00:46:40.129 --> 00:46:45.047
So I don't know, if you're in Germany,
you're on a phone call with somebody

00:46:45.047 --> 00:46:48.775
from India, and
then maybe there's somebody in Chicago or

00:46:48.775 --> 00:46:51.920
something like that,
take two or three minutes.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:53.674
So there's evidence to support this.

00:46:53.674 --> 00:46:57.066
Ask them where they're sitting,
ask them what the weather is like,

00:46:57.066 --> 00:46:58.946
ask them what their commute was like.

00:46:58.946 --> 00:47:03.233
Take a few minutes so to humanize and
to be able to get some empathy for them.

00:47:03.233 --> 00:47:06.275
But working in distributed
teams is really, really tough,

00:47:06.275 --> 00:47:08.435
but it's something that we all need to do.

00:47:08.435 --> 00:47:12.686
And to go back to the slack example where
so many of us who work at home now.

00:47:12.686 --> 00:47:17.120
That almost all of us, even when we,
in theory, all work in the same office,

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:20.486
we're often essentially
working on distributed teams.

00:47:20.486 --> 00:47:24.575
And the big warning from the research is
whenever you don't have eye contact, and

00:47:24.575 --> 00:47:27.905
this includes the telephone, so
what I'm doing to you right now.

00:47:27.905 --> 00:47:31.937
Whenever you don't have eye contact
with the people you're working with,

00:47:31.937 --> 00:47:36.162
that's when people start getting nastier
and start having empathy problems.

00:47:36.162 --> 00:47:36.830
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:39.578
So here's a good question that
actually I'm curious about as well.

00:47:39.578 --> 00:47:43.226
From reading your book, why are you so
obsessed with toxic enablers.

00:47:43.226 --> 00:47:43.881
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Toxic enablers, thank you.

00:47:43.881 --> 00:47:46.139
&gt;&gt; Toxic enablers, tell us about that.

00:47:46.139 --> 00:47:46.758
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you, sure.

00:47:46.758 --> 00:47:50.515
So toxic enablers, so
I've got this whole thing about,

00:47:50.515 --> 00:47:54.718
I don't want you to be part of
the solution, I mean part of the problem,

00:47:54.718 --> 00:47:56.906
I want you to be part of the solution.

00:47:56.906 --> 00:47:59.950
And for all of this, this notion that's
on you and you're not alone, it's on us,

00:47:59.950 --> 00:48:00.700
you're not alone.

00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:05.711
But what happens in
organizational settings?

00:48:05.711 --> 00:48:10.668
And so a lot of people who are out there
in companies, and the private sector,

00:48:10.668 --> 00:48:15.413
politics and so on who aren't ****,
but they clear the way for them.

00:48:15.413 --> 00:48:16.601
And they make it possible.

00:48:16.601 --> 00:48:21.272
And so my last book, The No **** Rule,
where I actually have a chapter,

00:48:21.272 --> 00:48:22.915
the virtues of ****.

00:48:22.915 --> 00:48:24.709
And I have a set of guidelines.

00:48:24.709 --> 00:48:27.383
If you want to be an effective ****.

00:48:27.383 --> 00:48:30.398
Make sure you've got somebody
to clean up after you,

00:48:30.398 --> 00:48:32.877
sort of like after
the parade comes to town,

00:48:32.877 --> 00:48:37.042
the people who sort of like clean up
the poop and the paper and everything.

00:48:37.042 --> 00:48:41.077
And old research by Peter Frost,
University of British Columbia.

00:48:41.077 --> 00:48:45.055
He identified these people,
toxic enablers, toxic handlers,

00:48:45.055 --> 00:48:49.702
who after the boss would scream at people,
after the boss would be abusive.

00:48:49.702 --> 00:48:54.246
What the toxic handler would do,
would go from office to office and

00:48:54.246 --> 00:48:59.358
tell the people who in this case he
abused, really he's not that bad a guy,

00:48:59.358 --> 00:49:02.465
he's not that mad, he'll cool down later.

00:49:02.465 --> 00:49:04.485
And then,
the other part of the toxic enabler,

00:49:04.485 --> 00:49:06.770
that they do is when the **** says,
was I that bad?

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:10.827
The toxic enabler, since there's a lot
of **** kissing involved in this, says,

00:49:10.827 --> 00:49:13.809
no no, you weren't really that bad,
and I talked to them,

00:49:13.809 --> 00:49:15.497
they're not really that upset.

00:49:15.497 --> 00:49:19.895
So, what you're doing is,
you may not be a jerk yourself, but

00:49:19.895 --> 00:49:24.640
you may be enabling somebody who
is doing all sorts of distraction.

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:27.018
And it's an interesting,
sort of weird role.

00:49:27.018 --> 00:49:31.874
And as I said, unfortunately,
the other side [LAUGH] if you want to be

00:49:31.874 --> 00:49:37.261
a successful jerk, hire yourself
a confident top high-strict enabler.

00:49:37.261 --> 00:49:39.673
So I hate to bring that up, but it's true.

00:49:39.673 --> 00:49:44.043
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So how do you, very quickly how
do you determine if you're dealing with

00:49:44.043 --> 00:49:48.903
a natural ****, or one who's just been
created by negative workplace dynamics.

00:49:48.903 --> 00:49:52.023
&gt;&gt; This question is sort of
&gt;&gt; Are **** sort of made or born?

00:49:52.023 --> 00:49:53.453
&gt;&gt; They do.

00:49:53.453 --> 00:49:57.274
&gt;&gt; So there is some evidence that people
who were bullies in high school go on to

00:49:57.274 --> 00:50:00.854
become bullies in the workplace and
they spend more time in prisons and

00:50:00.854 --> 00:50:01.901
all sorts of stuff.

00:50:01.901 --> 00:50:04.954
But I'm actually glad
you asked that question,

00:50:04.954 --> 00:50:09.718
because one thing I don't think I've
emphasized enough in this talk is that

00:50:09.718 --> 00:50:15.093
there's all sorts of situations that turn
almost all of us into temporary ****.

00:50:15.093 --> 00:50:20.191
And if you look at modern life, if
you're in a situation where your rushed,

00:50:20.191 --> 00:50:22.420
if it's crowded or if it's hot,

00:50:22.420 --> 00:50:27.539
if you are around other people who
are jerky, and income disparities.

00:50:27.539 --> 00:50:32.611
So we're in this income and status
disparity And to me the perfect analogy is

00:50:32.611 --> 00:50:38.021
getting on a modern airplane with first
class and all of the different statuses.

00:50:38.021 --> 00:50:41.886
United Airlines which
unfortunately I have to fly a lot.

00:50:41.886 --> 00:50:46.600
They have everything from business class,
first class to the obsolete I think you

00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:51.054
can't even put luggage in the overhead
compartment for the cheapest seats.

00:50:51.054 --> 00:50:55.221
And so what they do is they create
these status dynamics that sort

00:50:55.221 --> 00:50:57.237
of a microcosm of modern life.

00:50:57.237 --> 00:50:59.041
So, to me there's two parts.

00:50:59.041 --> 00:51:02.524
One is you may have some deep-seeded
personality characteristics like

00:51:02.524 --> 00:51:03.220
narcissism.

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:06.900
That make you very thin skinned and
very nasty but

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:12.663
you just might be in situations where
there is just, anybody would be a jerk.

00:51:12.663 --> 00:51:15.266
And there is some great experiments
done with seminary students.

00:51:15.266 --> 00:51:20.088
So this is like, the people
are training to be in the clergy.

00:51:20.088 --> 00:51:23.035
And they put them in situations
where they're in a rush and

00:51:23.035 --> 00:51:26.113
there's somebody lying on
the street who seems to be dying.

00:51:26.113 --> 00:51:29.747
When in a rush, they don't stop and
help them because they're in a rush.

00:51:29.747 --> 00:51:33.265
So, being in a hurry was one of the most
reliable way to turn into a jerk.

00:51:33.265 --> 00:51:37.080
But, yeah,
you might have personality problems.

00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:40.168
But beware of situations that can
turn almost anybody into a jerk.

00:51:40.168 --> 00:51:42.395
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you so much, Bob.

00:51:42.395 --> 00:51:45.637
I have to say we have a lot of
questions we weren't able to get to.

00:51:45.637 --> 00:51:48.786
You can absolutely see the value
of getting time with for

00:51:48.786 --> 00:51:53.287
experts like Bob here, and we just want to
thank you so much for joining us today.

00:51:53.287 --> 00:51:54.979
We hope you enjoyed the hour, and

00:51:54.979 --> 00:51:58.681
that you're better equipped now to
handle any jerks in your workplace.

00:51:58.681 --> 00:52:03.272
And we encourage you again, to check out
all of our offerings at scpd.stanford.edu.

00:52:03.272 --> 00:52:03.784
And again,

00:52:03.784 --> 00:52:07.586
you'll receive a recording of this webinar
to share with your friends and colleagues.

00:52:07.586 --> 00:52:08.848
We wish you a great rest of the day.

